The primary function of the control panel involves the selection of circuit breakers. For a typical building, emergency loads can be categorized into three types:
1. Fire-related loads, such as smoke exhaust fans, pressurization systems, elevators, fire pumps, and emergency lighting;
2. Non-fire-related loads that are part of the fire protection system;
3. Critical loads, such as those in hospitals or other essential facilities.

A generator circuit breaker panel is usually equipped with three or four output circuits:
- The first circuit supplies power to fire-related loads;
- The second circuit powers non-fire-related loads that are part of the fire protection system;
- The third circuit provides power to other critical loads;
- The fourth circuit (if configured) is used for load bank testing, allowing periodic performance checks of the generator without disrupting the normal operation of the main power system.

In data centers or server applications, the backup time may be half an hour or longer. However, if the UPS requires an excessively long backup time, it will face the issue of increased battery capacity demand, leading to larger space requirements. This is particularly evident in general commercial construction industries, as sufficient space may not be available to install large UPS equipment.

This is also the main difference between UPS and generators in terms of backup time, as briefly mentioned earlier.

Using UPS to power motor loads is relatively uncommon and typically only employed in special scenarios. In comparison, generator power supply offers significant advantages:

Firstly, its operation is more stable;

Secondly, it is more economical;

Thirdly, generators have greater capacity scalability.

